record_kind=per-invocation runner status record for one of the nine scanners (AAP 0.6.1)
record_filename_convention=<tool>.status, the name AAP 0.6.1 specifies; deliberately not the harness precedent's <tool>.meta.json
record_format=flat key=value, one pair per line, split on the first '='. That is the shape harness/lib/scope.sh scope_finish writes and harness/lib/normalize/cli.py _runner_status reads; the seven lines above are that record in its own key order and every line below extends it without displacing it
canonical_tool_identifier=trivy
canonical_tool_identifier_source=the AAP 0.5.4 class table, produced from the stem of the runner and its artifact rather than from a product name
scanner_class=per-record: vuln, secret or misconfig, taken from the section array the record was read from
scanner_class_is_single_value=false
scanner_class_varies_per_record=true
scanner_class_permitted_values=vuln,secret,misconfig
scanner_class_derivation=strictly from which of Results[].Vulnerabilities[], Results[].Secrets[] or Results[].Misconfigurations[] a record was read from - never from the record's own content
scanner_class_note=trivy is the single tool in the AAP 0.5.4 class table whose scanner_class is not fixed per tool, which is why this record carries a per-section breakdown rather than one class
scanner_class_observed=misconfig
scanner_class_derivation_verified=verified structurally, not assumed: one identical record body carrying VulnerabilityID, RuleID and ID at once was placed in each of the three sections in turn and yielded vuln, secret and misconfig respectively, with rule_id taken per section from the same body. The record's own content decided nothing
runner=/tmp/blitzy/blitzy-spark/blitzy-f38258d3-f87d-44f5-bedc-af512c69e0ab-w-016_e8e47c/harness/bin/run-trivy.sh
runner_repo_relative=harness/bin/run-trivy.sh
runner_absolute_path_derivation=the runner resolves its own location and derives the harness directory from it, so the log directory it printed at trivy.stdout.log line 4 identifies the checkout it ran in; the absolute path above is that checkout's harness/bin/run-trivy.sh, and it is this lane's own clone
runner_script_classification=runner
runner_inventory_context=harness/bin/ holds exactly the nine runners the AAP 0.5.4 class table names and no orchestrator
invoked_directly=true
invoked_with_arguments=none
invoked_one_at_a_time_within_clone=true
global_sequencing_satisfied=false
invoked_one_at_a_time_superseded_value=true
invoked_one_at_a_time_correction=The superseded value stated one-at-a-time without naming a scope, and no per-clone record has standing to state it globally - that is the fault being corrected here. One tool at a time holds only inside the clone this runner ran in (w-016_e8e47c). Measured across the run from the nine per-tool .status records' own start and finish stamps, this invocation (2026-08-25T00:50:38Z-00:51:00Z) overlapped NO other tool's window - it stands alone - so this invocation's own window is disjoint and the correction is made because the superseded value asserted a global property this invocation cannot establish, not because this window overlapped anything. AAP 0.8.1's requirement is GLOBAL - each runner invoked directly and individually, with its output captured before the next is started - and five pairs of other tools' windows do overlap, so this run records global sequencing as FAILED rather than satisfied. All five overlapping pairs are published once, in oss-scan-results/tool-status.md under "Facts common to all nine", and indexed in oss-scan-results/run-record.md section 8. semgrep.status records started_at and finished_at as not-established, so its window cannot be compared with any other and is excluded from the pair count rather than assumed disjoint. Nothing was measured afresh for this correction and nothing was repaired: no scanner was re-run, no window was re-measured, and the two fields above are read off the records already written.
invoked_through_orchestrator=false
invocation_discipline=invoked directly, with no arguments, never through an orchestrator (AAP 0.8.1), and one at a time WITHIN THIS CLONE; global sequencing across the run FAILED - see invocation_discipline_correction. An orchestrator's continue-on-error sequencing would carry the run past a condition that must halt it and would collapse per-tool attribution
invocation_discipline_superseded_value=invoked directly, with no arguments, one at a time, never through an orchestrator (AAP 0.8.1). An orchestrator's continue-on-error sequencing would carry the run past a condition that must halt it and would collapse per-tool attribution
invocation_discipline_correction=Direct invocation, no arguments and no orchestrator are unchanged: both were established by inspection of the runner and by this lane's own record. What the superseded value additionally asserted - one tool at a time - holds only inside the clone this runner ran in (w-016_e8e47c). Measured across the run from the nine per-tool .status records' own start and finish stamps, this invocation (2026-08-25T00:50:38Z-00:51:00Z) overlapped NO other tool's window - it stands alone. The global failure is therefore a property of the run rather than of this invocation, and it is recorded here because the superseded value asserted a global property this invocation cannot establish. AAP 0.8.1's requirement is GLOBAL - each runner invoked directly and individually, with its output captured before the next is started - so this run records global sequencing as FAILED rather than satisfied. All five overlapping pairs are published once, in oss-scan-results/tool-status.md under "Facts common to all nine", and indexed in oss-scan-results/run-record.md section 8. semgrep.status records started_at and finished_at as not-established, so its window cannot be compared with any other and is excluded from the pair count rather than assumed disjoint. Nothing here was repaired: no invocation was re-run to obtain a disjoint window.
argument_guard=run-trivy.sh lines 22-25 print 'run-trivy.sh: takes no arguments (configuration is baked in); refusing to scan' to stderr and exit 64
argument_guard_precedes_all_scanning_steps=true
argument_guard_established_by=inspection: the guard is the first executable statement, and environment sourcing (line 30), shared-library sourcing (32), target resolution (45) and the first tool invocation (67) all follow it
argument_guard_rejection_probe_performed=false
argument_guard_rejection_probe_reason=inspection settled the ordering, so no probe was needed; a blind probe against a defective runner could have performed a real scan and contaminated the artifact tree
started_at=2026-08-25T00:50:38Z
finished_at=2026-08-25T00:51:00Z
